{"title":"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 13","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Note\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Note</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>In the United States, coins have the following thicknesses: penny, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/a/6ba04c0269a26aebf635b1f82739f740ba40ed37.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1.55$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"31\" height=\"13\" /> mm; nickel, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/a/93ac9349f41267974c9a27522563a714ad027e36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1.95$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"31\" height=\"13\" /> mm; dime, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/3/97306b985de6c8e82811f9e1717c0e64d2bd7fbd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1.35$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"31\" height=\"13\" /> mm; quarter, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/a/90ab24f6f4655d2c0ca3284a9c75f2756049ee4c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1.75$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"31\" height=\"13\" /> mm. If a stack of these coins is exactly <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/0/820a20de2847dfdc1bc5cffc8d904933150032bd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$14$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> mm high, how many coins are in the stack?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/4/cb4e7b77220102e2127bbe7499e63588d3f7ef17.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathrm{(A) \\ } 7 \\qquad \\mathrm{(B) \\ } 8 \\qquad \\mathrm{(C) \\ } 9 \\qquad \\mathrm{(D) \\ } 10 \\qquad \\mathrm{(E) \\ } 11$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"359\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>All numbers in this solution will be in hundredths of a millimeter.\n</p><p>The thinnest coin is the dime, with thickness <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/e/d8efc4595ab8d0d7d43d5ec1988b1b7dc8ecc7fc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$135$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" />. A stack of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> dimes has height <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/c/57c598d169f0191944e719d1fa3238b0d0a38dcf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$135n$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"37\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p><p>The other three coin types have thicknesses <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/0/290a5eeae70aec9d0d8ba02dd3465ea57e43c9ad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$135+20$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"67\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/9/da98ff5546273fc8ae0833117a12e84f121e748c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$135+40$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"67\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/0/270a85e880f55f24f86979c461610e8be667e078.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$135+60$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"67\" height=\"14\" />. By replacing some of the dimes in our stack by other, thicker coins, we can clearly create exactly all heights in the set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/8/0a8b76a545d02a72324cf9f58d97b076ddcf878f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{135n, 135n+20, 135n+40, \\dots, 195n\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"307\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>If we take an odd <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, then all the possible heights will be odd, and thus none of them will be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/7/2d751cd0b479bceb949355d9a385b9b0fcddaa3b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1400$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"35\" height=\"13\" />. Hence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is even.\n</p><p>If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/7/6b781ecb692e190a0b96f3fb8b52653f6f21615a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n&lt;8$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"13\" /> the stack will be too low and if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/3/9c3b4036d87cbbed0983403a6adbb86fe1412efe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n&gt;10$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"52\" height=\"13\" /> it will be too high. Thus we are left with cases <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/4/1d4f171162b090bab7ce5dfa1c58715850530ba5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=8$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/c/dcc1b21210db29332b1a7d9ce088fd457f23aeee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"55\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/c/dcc1b21210db29332b1a7d9ce088fd457f23aeee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"55\" height=\"14\" /> the possible stack heights are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/a/9fa8239e0639bd68471cbaa2a98fe9185ed4d119.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1350,1370,1390,\\dots$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"153\" height=\"16\" />, with the remaining ones exceeding <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/7/2d751cd0b479bceb949355d9a385b9b0fcddaa3b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1400$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"35\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p><p>Therefore there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/e/5ce10d8b85a2bbad80aeed87a77c56aa145fa22f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\mathrm{(B)\\ }8}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"53\" height=\"29\" /> coins in the stack.\n</p><p>Using the above observation we can easily construct such a stack. A stack of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/8455f3b5cb3b4880b8c9d782a5c1f0334db819eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> dimes would have height <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/1/ec12182aa7ebf3e1aaa33004aad13b24037e2ab6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8\\cdot 135=1080$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"109\" height=\"13\" />, thus we need to add <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/6/0362f8decbd5131e34fd0619f907ecb8be327718.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$320$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" />.\nThis can be done for example by replacing five dimes by nickels (for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/4/0045a1057adbf1f16bcf246dc5d50c8bfa2e0fa5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$+60\\cdot 5 = +300$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"119\" height=\"14\" />), and one dime by a penny (for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/2/8/a2876c6ae005be23098b5c730b15313fe703a828.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$+20$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"34\" height=\"15\" />).\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/c/acc0e1defcd11c8124bf369766eb60897df6ac9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p,n,d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"48\" height=\"18\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/1/0615acc3725de21025457e7d6f7694dab8e2f758.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> be the number of pennies, nickels, dimes, and quarters used in the stack. \n</p><p>From the conditions above, we get the following equation:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/9/0/e90cd126f01ba1b511e60298c2b287dc3dbcdd3a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[155p+195n+135d+175q=1400.\\]\" width=\"276\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p>Then we divide each side by five to get\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/2/252854c806f1bf247a6f731481661e6e500be9db.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[31p+39n+27d+35q=280.\\]\" width=\"231\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p>Writing both sides in terms of mod 4, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/9/9f91f3d3efadecd9133eb24bc2ae9e50704b3c17.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-p-n-d-q \\equiv 0  \\pmod 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"223\" height=\"18\" />. \n</p><p>This means that the sum <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/c/f7c7557d8f5652aed7a974052c11281b41e259f1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p+n+d+q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"105\" height=\"16\" /> is divisible by 4. Therefore, the answer must be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/e/a7ef8595c8039667a688f1f4512e6f3bb87f7595.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{(B)\\,\\, 8}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"62\" height=\"32\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We notice that the thickness of 4 quarters is 7 mm. 7 is half of 14, so we multiply the 4 quarters by two and get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/4/a645df22dcc6d2a874e49968f57f5d26d0218527.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{(B)\\,\\, 8}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"57\" height=\"32\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Note\">Note</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Note\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We can easily add up <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/3/9a30142e8532294a0a125171e1506cb32171befc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1.55\\text{\\ mm}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"65\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/8/02819482157c7b5eb295b60e31fb58d1994ad677.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1.95\\text{\\ mm}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"65\" height=\"13\" /> to get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/8/e/28e4117f7e90067290b39c1ae3d349ef6d825f83.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3.50\\text{\\ mm}$\" width=\"65\" height=\"12\" />. We multiply that by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/2/2/7223f840af98b5886fb73c4209d87dc27588da3e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$14\\text{\\ mm}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"51\" height=\"12\" />. Since this works and it requires 8 coins, the answer is clearly <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/e/5ce10d8b85a2bbad80aeed87a77c56aa145fa22f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\mathrm{(B)\\ }8}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"53\" height=\"29\" />.\n</p><p>Similarly, we can simply take <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/8455f3b5cb3b4880b8c9d782a5c1f0334db819eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> quarters to get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/a/f5ab2600b1a9ba810c8965369dcc6a7a2491ef12.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8\\cdot 1.75=14$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"97\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B\">2004 AMC 10B</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Answer_Key\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c13\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_12\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 12\">Problem 12</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_14\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 14\">Problem 14</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_5\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 6\">6</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_7\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 7\">7</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_8\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 8\">8</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_9\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 9\">9</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_10\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 10\">10</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_11\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 11\">11</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_12\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 12\">12</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">13</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_14\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 14\">14</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_15\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 15\">15</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_16\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 16\">16</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_17\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 17\">17</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_18\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 18\">18</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_19\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 19\">19</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_20\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 20\">20</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_21\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 21\">21</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_22\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 22\">22</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_23\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 23\">23</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_24\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 24\">24</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_25\" title=\"2004 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 25\">25</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=AMC_10_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"AMC 10 Problems and Solutions\"> All AMC 10 Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center\">\n<div>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC_Logo.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/d1/AMC_Logo.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"97\" height=\"84\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex-grow:1;text-align:right\">\n<p>These problems are copyrighted &#169; by the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.maa.org\">Mathematical Association of America</a>. \n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"clear:both;\"></div>\n</div></div>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017143946\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.049 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.068 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 329/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 2324/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 158/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 8086/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%   13.451      1 -total\n 58.44%    7.861      1 Template:AMC10_box\n 40.64%    5.466      1 Template:MAA_Notice\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:7707:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017143946 and revision id 228689. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}